          "note": "Fig. 2 right (in situ Raman, 1 wt % Cu/SiO₂ at 230 °C, 200 and 400 bar; Renishaw InVia, 532 nm Nd:YAG, 60 mW, 600 s accumulation through a micro-view-cell, SI p. S34). Marked on the figure with a filled circle as a band of the reactant mixture rather than a surface species, the stronger, upper member of the CO₂ Fermi dyad, which is what a Raman spectrum of CO₂ shows in this window. No position is printed for the marker, so none is recorded."
        },
        {
          "state": "gas",
          "key": "Fehr.SpectroscopicSignaturesPressurized.2020",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "wn": 1388,
          "note": "Table 3 and Fig. 3: formally infrared-inactive, yet observed in DRIFTS of pure KBr once the CO₂ pressure passes roughly 10 bar, together with its Fermi partner at 1285 cm⁻¹ and with an intensity ratio near 1.0 at 30 bar. The pair is the Fermi resonance itself made visible: the deperturbed ν₁ lies at 1337 cm⁻¹ and is pushed up to 1388, while 2ν₂ falls from 1334 to 1285 cm⁻¹. Both grow with pressure and, unlike every combination band in the same spectrum, weaken as the temperature rises."
        },
        {
          "state": "gas",
          "key": "Urakawa.OnchipRamanAnalysis.2008",
          "laser_nm": 785,
          "technique": "raman",
          "wn": 1387,
          "note": "Text and Fig. 2: the upper Fermi dyad band at ca. 1387 cm⁻¹, neat CO₂ flowed through a Si/glass microreactor chip, 785 nm excitation, 5–14 MPa at 298, 307 and 323 K, so across the gas, liquid and supercritical states. The position depends on pressure and temperature: read off Fig. 2, it runs from about 1389 cm⁻¹ (5 MPa, 323 K) down to about 1386 cm⁻¹ in the dense fluid, stepping abruptly at the gas–liquid (298 K, 6–7 MPa) and gas–supercritical (307 K, 7–8 MPa) transitions and falling smoothly at 323 K. The paper calls the pair the Fermi dyad without saying which component is ν₁; the split follows the atlas. With 5 % each of cyclohexene and H₂ (10 MPa, 305–318 K) the dyad sits at or below the liquid value at 305 K, which the paper reads as phase segregation."
        }
      ],
