        {
          "key": "Davydov.MolecularSpectroscopyOxide.2003",
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Section 2.3.2, p. 96 of a monograph, not one measurement: bridged carbonyls lie below 1900 cm⁻¹ and three-coordinated ones below 1800. The book adds a constraint the position alone does not give: a carbonyl on a cation can only be linear, because in an M–O–M arrangement the cations are too far apart for one CO molecule to bond to two of them at once. A bridged band is therefore evidence of an ensemble of reduced metal atoms rather than of a cation, whatever else is on the surface. Where a cation has two free coordination places it forms a dicarbonyl instead."
        }
