        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2125,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Fig. 3a (KRS-5 transmission cell, −160 °C, 0.15 mbar CO, static vacuum): a Cu⁺-CO feature absent from the unpromoted Cu/SiO₂ and appearing only once Ga is loaded. Its deconvoluted area gives the Cu⁺/(Cu⁰+Cu⁺) ratio, which rises linearly with Ga content (Fig. S4a) and correlates with TOF(MeOH) at every temperature with R² above 0.95 (Fig. 3b). Assigned to Cu⁺ at the Cu-Ga₂O₃ interface, the proposed methanol active site.",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2147,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Figs. S3a and S3b (DRIFTS, 5% CO/He flowing at 20 mL min⁻¹, 1 bar): the same Cu⁺ carbonyl at high coverage under flowing CO, 22 cm⁻¹ above the value the transmission experiment gives at −160 °C under vacuum. The paper attributes the whole difference to coverage and to static against dynamic conditions rather than to a different site, noting that the Cu⁰ to Cu⁺ separation holds near 27 cm⁻¹ in both. Well defined at 220 °C only on CuGa(0.15) and CuGa(0.20); desorption is followed in Figs. S5 and S6.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2196,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Figs. S3c, S4b and S10 (DRIFTS, 10% CO₂/Ar at 20 mL min⁻¹, 3 bar, 220 °C): no CO is fed here. The band grows when CO₂ alone is dosed, and the authors read it as Cu⁺(O)-CO, the carbonyl left on a Cu⁺ that has just split CO₂ into CO* and O*. It is absent on the unpromoted catalyst and its area scales with Ga loading (Fig. S4b), which is their evidence that the interfacial Cu⁺ dissociates CO₂ without hydrogen. Switching to H₂/Ar moves it to 2165 cm⁻¹ (Fig. S10), read as the oxygen being taken off the site.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2165,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Table S5 and Fig. S10 (operando DRIFTS, 220 °C, H₂/CO₂ = 3, 6 bar, 25 mL min⁻¹): Cu⁺-CO under working conditions, and the band the 2196 cm⁻¹ Cu⁺(O)-CO turns into once H₂ reaches the surface. This is the carbonyl the paper's RWGS plus CO-hydrogenation route runs through, so its presence under reaction rather than under a CO probe is the point of the measurement. Literature values quoted alongside it are 2166 to 2173 cm⁻¹.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": []
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2190,
          "measured_on": ["cu_2p", "cu_sio2"],
          "note": "Table S5: assigned to Cu²⁺-CO, against a literature value of 2199 cm⁻¹. The table lists its feed condition as CO₂+H₂, but the only figures showing the band are the formic acid and methanol adsorption experiments over Cu/SiO₂ (Figs. S15a and S16a), so the table and the figures do not agree on which experiment it came from and the sample is recorded as the one those figures name. A Cu²⁺ carbonyl on a catalyst reduced at 300 °C is a minority claim, and the paper neither argues it further nor uses it.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "key": "Zdravkova.SurprisingCoordinationChemistry.2015",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 2158,
          "measured_on": "cu_1p",
          "note": "Monocarbonyl Cu⁺-CO in a Cu-exchanged zeolite, resolved by self-supporting-pellet transmission FTIR (100 K) into 4 distinct Cu⁺ site sub-families: 2166 cm⁻¹ (most electrophilic cationic site, preferentially covered by co-adsorbed H₂O), 2158 cm⁻¹ (principal/dominant cationic site), 2141 cm⁻¹ (a more highly-coordinated, oxide-like Cu⁺ site), and 2139 cm⁻¹ (Cu⁺(H₂O)(CO) mixed-ligand complex, nearly coincident with the oxide-like site). Site assignment supported by selective monocarbonyl→dicarbonyl→tricarbonyl conversion as CO coverage increases/temperature decreases — see co_gemdi_asym/co_gemdi_sym for the dicarbonyl stage.",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "key": "Elgayyar.ContributionsLimitationsIR.2021",
          "wn": [
            2189,
            2172,
            2160,
            2134
          ],
          "measured_on": [
            "ag_1p",
            "au_1p",
            "au_ag_silicalite"
          ],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. 5A, p. 63 (Au, Au-Ag and Ag embedded in silicalite-1, operando DRIFTS at 100 °C under 2.8 % CO + 8 % O₂ in He): Ag⁺-CO at 2189 and 2172 cm⁻¹ on the plain silver sample moves to about 2160 on the alloy, and the Au⁺-CO signal at 2134 falls by half with the gold loading and particle size unchanged. The two together are the review's evidence for a genuinely alloyed phase. Silver has the lower surface tension of the pair, 1086 against 1333 mJ m⁻², so it takes the corner and edge sites and displaces the gold that would otherwise sit there."
        },
        {
          "key": "Davydov.MolecularSpectroscopyOxide.2003",
          "wn": 2190,
          "measured_on": [
            "zn_2p",
            "zno"
          ],
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Section 2.3.2, pp. 95–96 and Table 2.11, p. 99 (a monograph; the sampling geometry behind the number is not stated). One band at 2190 cm⁻¹ after CO adsorption on ZnO at 293 K, with photoelectron work on the same system showing the bond is made through the carbon and the 5σ orbital. On a coordinatively unsaturated cation the bond is essentially σ only, so ν(CO) sits above the 2143 cm⁻¹ of the free molecule, and the shift upward grows with the cation's ratio of charge to radius. Such complexes are relatively unstable and are usually removed by degassing at room temperature, which is one way to tell them from the far more stable M⁺ and M⁰ carbonyls (p. 274)."
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "s",
      "width": "sharp",
      "confidence": "confirmed",
      "tags": ["site-sensitive"]
    },
