      "wn_start": 2105,
      "wn_end": 2125,
      "short": "ν(CO) iso (M-OC)",
      "description": "CO coordinated through its oxygen rather than its carbon, the reverse orientation of every other CO band here. On an electrostatic cation site with no backdonation the field polarises the bond the other way, so O-bonding puts ν(CO) below the free-gas value while C-bonding on the same site puts it above. The two orientations coexist as close-lying isomers, with the C-bonded one preferred and far stronger.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "key": "Ugliengo.QuantumChemicalCalculations.1999",
          "wn": 2108,
          "measured_on": ["li_1p", "zsm5"],
          "technique": "transmission",
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "note": "Table 1, the measured LF band on LiZSM-5 (Si/Al = 14), from self-supporting wafers activated at 723 K in vacuo and dosed with about 1 Torr of CO at liquid-nitrogen temperature. Below the 2143 cm⁻¹ of free CO, and it rises as the cation grows while the HF band falls; its intensity relative to the HF band grows steadily from Li to Cs, which with the calculations is the paper's evidence that this is CO bound through its oxygen.",
