      "atoms": "C-O",
      "wn_start": 2020,
      "wn_end": 2160,
      "short": "νₐₛ(CO) gem-M⁺(CO)₂",
      "description": "Asymmetric ν(CO) component of a geminal dicarbonyl species: two linear CO ligands bound to a single isolated cationic metal center, stretching out of phase (one lengthens as the other shortens). The symmetric/asymmetric pair appearing together, rather than a single linear-CO band, is itself diagnostic of an atomically dispersed cationic site — a single metal atom isolated enough to bind two CO molecules at once. Typical splitting between the pair is ~50–60 cm⁻¹.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "key": "Zdravkova.SurprisingCoordinationChemistry.2015",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 2151,
          "measured_on": "cu_1p",
          "note": "νₐₛ(CO) of Cu⁺(CO)₂ geminal dicarbonyl in a Cu-exchanged zeolite at 2151 cm⁻¹ (paired with νₛ at 2180, see co_gemdi_sym), forming from the monocarbonyl with increasing CO coverage. The geminal (one-cation-two-CO) assignment was proven unambiguously by adsorbing a 1:1 ¹²CO+¹³CO isotopic mixture: a force-field calculation from the pure-¹²CO dicarbonyl frequencies predicted mixed-isotope Cu⁺(¹²CO)(¹³CO) bands at 2169/2113 cm⁻¹ and Cu⁺(¹³CO)₂ bands at 2131/2103 cm⁻¹, and both were observed exactly as calculated — only explainable if two CO ligands genuinely share one cation and vibrationally couple, ruling out two coincidentally-overlapping monocarbonyl bands from different sites.",
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling", "direct-dosing"]
        }
      ],
      "tags": ["site-sensitive", "to-be-revised"],
      "vibration_modes": ["co_geminal_stretch_asymmetric"]
    },
