      "wn_start": 1960,
      "wn_end": 2015,
      "short": "ν(Ga-H)",
      "description": "Terminal hydride on a gallium centre: H₂ dissociated on the oxide itself rather than on a metal particle. Heterolytic splitting across a Ga-O pair puts a hydride on Ga and a proton on lattice oxygen, so Ga-H and fresh O-H bands appear together; the two gallium coordinations split it by about 25 cm⁻¹. On a reduced surface H₂ can split homolytically instead, into two hydrides and no new O-H, so the hydroxyl is not a count of the hydride. It sits in the CO stretching window, which is the standing confusion, and deuteration to near 1435 cm⁻¹ settles it. Metal-H absorbs far more weakly than O-H, so intensity is a poor guide to coverage.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "AlAbdulghani.UncoveringPressureDependentMechanism.2025",
          "wn": 1976,
          "measured_on": ["ga", "gazrox"],
          "note": "Fig. 4b to 4d (ME-DRIFTS, Cu-free GaZrOₓ, 1 bar and 20 bar, 260 °C): ν(Ga-H) at 1976 cm⁻¹, strong on the oxide alone (about 0.03 Kubelka-Munk in Fig. 4d). The assignment is confirmed by feeding D₂ in place of H₂: the band is absent under CO₂+D₂ (Fig. 4c). No Ga-D counterpart is reported anywhere in the paper or its SI, so the evidence is the disappearance, not a measured shift. The band stays largely static through the modulation, which the authors read as a slow step: Ga-H reduces (bi)carbonates to HCOO* and helps hydrogenate HCOO* to CH₃O*.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling"]
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "AlAbdulghani.UncoveringPressureDependentMechanism.2025",
          "wn": 1980,
          "measured_on": ["ga", "cu_gazrox"],
          "note": "Fig. 1b (ME-DRIFTS-PSD, Cu-GaZrOₓ, 20 bar, 260 °C): the same hydride on the Cu-containing sample, marked at 1980 cm⁻¹, four wavenumbers above the value quoted for the Cu-free oxide. It is far weaker here, near 0.001 against 0.03 Kubelka-Munk on GaZrOₓ (Fig. 4d), which is the paper's argument that Ga-H accumulates on the oxide and is consumed quickly once Cu is present.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": []
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "wn": 2011,
          "measured_on": ["ga_tet", "ga2o3"],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling"],
          "note": "Fig. 1 (in-situ DRIFTS, precipitated Ga₂O₃, 0.1 MPa H₂ at 450 °C, referenced to the same sample under Ar): the weaker, higher component of the hydride pair, assigned to H on tetrahedrally coordinated gallium together with the O-H at 3685 cm⁻¹. The pair is split by about 25 cm⁻¹, which the authors attribute to the two Ga coordinations of the bulk oxide rather than to two different kinds of hydride. Their own DFT cuts against this component: it finds the four-fold surface Ga saturated and Ga(t)-H formation impossible, leaving only the unsaturated Ga(o) able to take a hydrogen. The paper does not reconcile the two."
        },
        {
          "state": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "wn": 1988,
          "measured_on": ["ga_oct", "ga2o3"],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling"],
          "note": "Fig. 1: the dominant component of the hydride pair on precipitated Ga₂O₃ under 0.1 MPa H₂ at 450 °C, assigned to H on octahedrally coordinated gallium with the O-H at 3532 cm⁻¹. Formation needs temperature: the band is weak at room temperature and grows monotonically above 150 °C (Fig. 2a), matching the onset of H₂-D₂ exchange. Ar purging removes it within 30 min at 450 °C and it returns on re-admitting H₂, so the dissociation is reversible (Figs. S5, S6). Coverage tracks the pretreatment, O₂ < Ar < H₂ (Fig. S9). It is inert to CO₂, CO, O₂, N₂ and Ar at 150 °C; at 200 °C both O₂ and CO₂ consume it, giving water and formate, while N₂ and CO leave it untouched even above 450 °C (Fig. 4)."
        }
