      "atoms": "H-H",
      "wn_start": 802,
      "wn_end": 826,
      "short": "θ(H₂)",
      "description": "One line of the pure rotational Raman spectrum of hydrogen, the transition from J = 2 to J = 4. Nothing vibrates: the molecule only turns faster, and Raman reaches the change directly through the polarizability. Only ΔJ = +2 arises for pure rotation, since the upper level is by definition the higher one, so this spectrum is an S branch and nothing else. Hydrogen spreads it over more than 2000 cm⁻¹, with lines about 180 cm⁻¹ apart, which is why they are carried one by one.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "key": "Kojima.QuantitativeAnalysisSpectral.2005",
          "technique": "srs",
          "laser_nm": 532,
          "state": "gas",
          "note": "Fig. 6 labels this line in the pure rotational H₂ spectrum, measured by spontaneous Raman in a 10 atm H₂-air flame; the paper takes the flame temperature from the intensity distribution of these lines, 1425 K in Fig. 6. The line separation across the branch is about 180 cm⁻¹. The laser line is not stated, only a pulsed Nd:YAG; 532 nm is inferred from the figure axes.",
          "tags": []
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "w",
      "width": "sharp",
      "confidence": "likely",
      "tags": ["ir-inactive"]
